\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Remark}
\title{Stationary elimination and the tree contribution}
\date{2026-10-30}

\p{For the [three-coordinate quartic action](fdip-0047), minimizing
the eliminated variables and integrating their fluctuations agree
on some coefficients but not all. Write #{u=(y,z)},
#{u_0=(x,x)} and
##{H=C^{-1}=\begin{pmatrix}1&1\\1&2\end{pmatrix}.}
At fixed #{x}, the action is
##{\mathcal A(x,u)=\frac{x^2}{2}
 +\frac12(u-u_0)^TC(u-u_0)+\lambda V(x,u).}
There is a unique formal stationary solution
#{u_*(x,\lambda)=u_0+O(\lambda)}. Its stationary value is
##{\begin{aligned}
\mathcal A_{\mathrm{cl}}(x,\lambda)
 &=\mathcal A(x,u_*(x,\lambda))\\
 &=\frac{x^2}{2}+\frac{\lambda x^4}{8}
    -\frac{5\lambda^2x^6}{72}+O(\lambda^3).
\end{aligned}}
For real #{\lambda\geq0}, the action is coercive and strictly
convex in #{u}: the quadratic Hessian #{C} is positive definite
and the quartic summands are convex. It therefore has a unique
minimizer. The calculation below uses only formal stationarity
at #{\lambda=0}.}

\proof{
\p{The equation is
#{C(u-u_0)+\lambda\nabla_uV(x,u)=0}.
Since #{C} is invertible, comparison of successive powers of
#{\lambda} uniquely determines the formal coefficients. Put
##{r=\nabla_uV(x,u_0)=\frac{x^3}{6}\binom{1}{1}.}
The first coefficient gives #{u_*=u_0-\lambda Hr+O(\lambda^2)}.
Substitution in the action yields
##{\mathcal A_{\mathrm{cl}}=
 \frac{x^2}{2}+\lambda V(x,u_0)
 -\frac{\lambda^2}{2}r^THr+O(\lambda^3).}
Now #{V(x,u_0)=x^4/8} and #{r^THr=5x^6/36}. This proves the
stationary coefficient without replacing a Gaussian integral by
a minimum.}
}

\p{To distinguish fluctuation terms, take #{\hbar>0}, let
#{\eta_\hbar} be centered Gaussian with covariance #{\hbar H}, and
use the normalized fiber expression
##{F_{\lambda,\hbar}(x)=
 -\hbar\log\mathbb E\exp\left(
 -\frac{\lambda}{\hbar}V(x,u_0+\eta_\hbar)\right).}
Expand formally in #{\lambda}; through the second order its
coefficients are polynomials in #{\hbar}:
##{\begin{aligned}
F_{\lambda,\hbar}(x)
={}&\lambda\left(\frac{x^4}{8}+\frac{3\hbar x^2}{4}
                    +\frac{5\hbar^2}{8}\right)\\
 &-\lambda^2\left(\frac{5x^6}{72}
                    +\frac{53\hbar x^4}{48}\right.\\
 &\hspace{38pt}\left.+\frac{97\hbar^2x^2}{24}
                    +\frac{41\hbar^3}{24}\right)
 +O(\lambda^3).
\end{aligned}}
Indeed each Gaussian pairing contributes one #{\hbar}; the first
coefficient is #{\mathbb E V} and the second is
#{-\operatorname{Var}(V)/(2\hbar)}. Applying this scaling to the
explicit moment calculation proves the formula.}

\p{Consequently, through order #{\lambda^2}, the coefficient of
#{\hbar^0} in this fiber interaction is
##{\begin{aligned}
[\hbar^0]F_{\lambda,\hbar}(x)
 &=\frac{\lambda x^4}{8}-\frac{5\lambda^2x^6}{72}
     +O(\lambda^3)\\
 &=\mathcal A_{\mathrm{cl}}(x,\lambda)-\frac{x^2}{2}
     +O(\lambda^3).
\end{aligned}}
The retained Gaussian action #{x^2/2} must be added to recover
the full stationary value. At #{\lambda=0} the normalized fiber
interaction is zero, whereas that stationary value is #{x^2/2}.}

\p{This is the \newvocab{tree contribution} in the loop grading of
\citet{v2, Section 3.6 and Theorem 3.12, pp. 38-39}{etingof2024mathematical}.
In that convention a Gaussian contraction contributes #{\hbar}
and an interaction vertex contributes #{\hbar^{-1}}. The logarithm
selects connected graphs; after multiplication by #{-\hbar},
a connected graph with #{e} contracted edges and #{v} interaction
vertices has power #{\hbar^{e-v+1}}. Its number of independent
cycles is #{e-v+1}; zero cycles means a tree. Thus connected
contractions need not be trees. In the displayed second-order
coefficient, only the sextic term is at tree order; its quartic,
quadratic and constant companions have positive powers of
#{\hbar}. The source's stationary-action convention is applied to
the shifted fiber action, whose unperturbed minimum is zero,
which explains the subtraction of #{x^2/2}.}
